    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## The 2-sphere $S^2$\n\nSame idea: stereographic projection from $(0, 0, 1)$ and $(0, 0, -1)$ gives two charts covering $S^2$. The transition map (in the equatorial annulus) is *complex inversion* $1/z$ — same flavour as $S^1$.\n\n**Why two charts?** No single chart can cover all of $S^n$ — a sphere is *compact* but $\\mathbb{R}^n$ isn't, so a continuous bijection between them is impossible. Two charts is the minimum.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "ax_draw3d(\n  parametric_surface(cos(u)*sin(v), sin(u)*sin(v), cos(v),\n                     u, 0, 2*%pi, v, 0, %pi),\n  title=\"S² with two stereographic charts (cover all but two opposing poles)\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"y\", zlabel=\"z\",\n  colorscale=\"Viridis\"\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 23,
      "outputs": [
