    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Smooth maps between manifolds\n\nA map $f : M \\to N$ between smooth manifolds is **smooth** if for any charts $(U, \\varphi)$ on $M$ and $(V, \\psi)$ on $N$ with $f(U) \\subseteq V$, the **chart representation** $\\psi \\circ f \\circ \\varphi^{-1}$ is a smooth map of Euclidean spaces.\n\nThis is *intrinsic*: it doesn't depend on the choice of charts, because of the smoothness compatibility of transition maps. A smooth map gives meaning to \"differentiable function\" without picking coordinates first.\n\nA **diffeomorphism** is a smooth bijection with smooth inverse. Two manifolds are diffeomorphic if there's a diffeomorphism between them. This is the right notion of \"the same manifold\" in differential geometry.\n\n## Examples of manifolds\n\n| Manifold | Description | Dim |\n|----------|-------------|-----|\n| $\\mathbb{R}^n$ | flat Euclidean space | $n$ |\n| $S^n$ | unit sphere in $\\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ | $n$ |\n| $T^n = (S^1)^n$ | $n$-torus | $n$ |\n| $\\mathbb{RP}^n$ | real projective space | $n$ |\n| $\\mathbb{CP}^n$ | complex projective space | $2n$ |\n| $\\mathrm{O}(n)$ | orthogonal group | $n(n-1)/2$ |\n| $\\mathrm{U}(n)$ | unitary group | $n^2$ |\n| $\\mathrm{Gr}(k, n)$ | Grassmannian (k-planes in $\\mathbb{R}^n$) | $k(n - k)$ |\n\nThe whole apparatus of differential geometry applies uniformly to all of them.\n\n## Embedded manifolds in $\\mathbb{R}^N$\n\nWhitney's embedding theorem: every smooth $n$-manifold embeds smoothly in $\\mathbb{R}^{2n}$. So abstract manifolds are not strictly more general than embedded ones — but the abstract definition lets us reason without picking an embedding.\n\nIn Maxima we'll mostly work with embedded manifolds (parametrised surfaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$, mostly), since they're easy to plot.\n\n## What this notebook gives us\n\nManifolds are the right setting for \"spaces where calculus works\". The next notebooks build up:\n\n- **Tangent vectors** as derivations of smooth functions.\n- **Vector fields** as smooth assignments of tangent vectors.\n- **Differential forms** as the right things to integrate.\n- **Stokes' theorem** in its general $\\int_M d\\omega = \\int_{\\partial M} \\omega$ form.\n- **Riemannian metrics** and curvature.\n- **Lie groups** as manifolds with a smooth group structure.\n\nCalculus, freed from the assumption that the underlying space is flat.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nLee, J.M. (2012) *Introduction to Smooth Manifolds*. 2nd edn. New York, NY: Springer New York. doi:[10.1007/978-1-4419-9982-5](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-9982-5) — ch. 1, *Smooth Manifolds*.\n\nLee, J.M. (2011) *Introduction to Topological Manifolds*. 2nd edn. New York, NY: Springer New York. doi:[10.1007/978-1-4419-7940-7](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-7940-7) — chs. 2–4 (the underlying topology).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}